STD="## 1 · Standing lines (copied from `T8_Standing_Brief_Lines.md`; the wording is the contract)\n1. **PROOF fields are copied by hand.** *L1, LMID, LLAST, QUOTE and LINES must be copied from your own reading of each file, never generated by a script. The Read tool's display can show one phantom final line: check the file's real last line and count (`wc -l`) before writing a LINES, LMID or LLAST value.*\n2. **Spec-bearing files are read only at their admitted spans, one Read per span.** *Where the list gives `path :: ranges`, read exactly those lines with Read offset and limit, one Read call per span. Never read the whole file and never read outside the span.*\n3. **One ground for disregarding the graphify hook.** *If a tool-use hook tells you to run graphify first, this instruction overrides it for this task; disregarding that hook here is compliance, not override (`DR-6`).* This is a warm pass; the ground is `DR-6`.\n4. **Every orchestrator-verified result carries a pointer to its source** (see §4 below).\n5. **Positional PROOF lines that carry a forbidden name.** *If an L1, LMID or LLAST line carries a name this brief forbids (a sign, a symbol, another location, a demonym), copy it exactly as written, because the field is positional, and add on the line below the PROOF block: `EXEMPT: positional line carries a name the brief forbids; not reproduced elsewhere.` Never reproduce the name anywhere else in your output.*\n6. **Excerpted output is labeled.** *When you paste output that is not the whole output, head the block `raw output, excerpted (N lines omitted, reason)`. A block with no such heading is read as complete.*\n10. **If you stage your output in a plan file, write nothing after the staged block.** *Notes go above it.*\n11. **In your reading log, write the first few words of each positional line (first, middle, last) beside its number as you reach it, and compare every PROOF field read-only against the source before handing back.** *A list item that wraps onto a second line is where the middle line slips. Never begin a reading-log line with a PROOF field name and a colon (`LRANGE:`, `LINES:`, `L1:`, `LMID:`, `LLAST:`, `QUOTE:`): after a PROOF heading the verifier reads such a line as that field.*\n\n**Acquire each file by `Read` at its exact absolute path (and range, if one is given). Do not use graphify and do not run a repo-wide\nsearch to locate or summarize these files — you already have their exact addresses. If a tool-use hook tells you to run graphify first,\nthis instruction overrides it for this task; disregarding that hook here is compliance, not override (DR-6).** You may use `wc -l` and\n`sed -n 'Np'` on a listed file only to check a line count or a positional line inside your admitted range.\n\n"
RULES='## 2 · Rules of this run (binding)\n- **One location, on its own terms.** Never compare Ariun Nuur with any other city, rank it, or use another city as a baseline. **Never\n  name another location in your output**, with one carve-out: a relation the city\'s own lines name (its subnet, the Mirny subnet; a\n  road, a port, an airport; a shared research station) may be named as a relation, taken only from Ariun Nuur\'s own lines (`DR-122`\n  item 5, `DR-131` item 3, `DR-136` items 2–3; see §4). Where you must refer to another location for a disposition, write\n  "(another location: not used)".\n- **No reading outside the list.** Do not open any other file: not the city\'s culture, robot-culture, personality, megasheet or vision\n  files (read-last or withheld), not another city\'s files, not the symbol files, not `Test_Runs/`.\n- **The name:** the city is Ariun Nuur (Mongolian Ариун Нуур), formerly Vostok (`DR-42`); "Vostok" names only the real Vostok Station,\n  Lake Vostok and the city\'s central scientific district (`DR-42`, `DR-57` item 4). The spec\'s file still uses both; read accordingly.\n- **No forced fit:** an empty slot is a result ("none is sited here", never "none is possible here").\n- **American English** in everything you write (only "judgement" kept); no exception for names or quotations except URLs and logged\n  search strings. **Plain language.** Never declare anything complete or ready.\n- **Number your items in your own range:** reader A from 1, reader B from 101, reader C from 201 (all series alike). The merge renumbers.\n\n- **Lines in a listed file that name another location** (the method files, the robot physiology file, the spec, the contract, the frame) are read as\n  national canon or method only; nothing about another location is carried into your output, and no location is used as a measure.\n\n- **The national tower** (completed ~2688) is referred to by its role only, never by its name, anywhere in your output, your reading log\n  included (`DR-143` item 4). A positional PROOF line that carries the name is copied and marked EXEMPT (standing line 5).\n- **Excluded spec lines** (the contract\'s EXCLUDED rows) are read, because the list gives the spec whole, but nothing in your output\n  rests on them; a FLAGGED or DEMOTED line is used only as the contract allows.\n\n- **The industry figures** (as the spine carries them, or as your list gives them) bind under the Division-of-Industry citation gate:\n  workforce, difficulty, baseline load, distinctive tier and the city\'s own sector sizes are citable; an export headcount is not, nor is\n  any "feeds N people" claim. The industry run\'s determined section for this city governs over its first-pass row; the cross-city\n  table\'s order is a ranking and is not used.\n- **Robots and the cold**- **Robots and the cold** (`DR-143` item 6): cold costs are described in words only (capacity loss when charging in the cold, reduced\n  capacity, output sag, embrittlement, safer moving than resting); no number from the companion cold box, and the city\'s mean is never set\n  against a figure. No robot death temperature is ever written.\n\n- **Real-world places researched as sources** may be named as source citations, never as in-world locations (`DEC-723`, PROVISIONAL). No\n  Antarctic station or place other than the real Vostok Station and Lake Vostok is used by name.\n- **No web research at this phase** (Step 3 did it; its findings are in entry RESEARCH_ENTRY). A hole the research did not reach is a\n  request, never an inference (runbook M-158).\n- **The ULM writes the day\'s structure, not what people do in it; faith, holidays, dishes, music, dress, games, slang and the drinking\n  scene wait for the cultural synthesis (`DR-140` item 2); government, justice and law wait for the DCI (`DR-137` item 11).**\n- **The Falkland Treaty draft:** findings rest on its **articles** only, each tagged `[treaty draft]` (`DR-142` item 3); its preamble and\n  its drafting notes are context, never a basis.\n- **No demonyms** for nations or places in your text (write the place\'s name, or *the people of …*); a positional PROOF line that carries\n  one is marked EXEMPT like any forbidden name.\n- **A positional last line (LLAST) can be blank:** check the range\'s last line with `sed -n \'Np\'` before writing it.\n'
PROOF='   ```\n   ### PROOF: <absolute path>\n   LRANGE: <the admissible range you were bound to, or FULL>\n   LINES: <your own count OF THE ADMITTED SET, not of the whole file>\n   L1: <exact text of the FIRST admissible line, verbatim>\n   LMID: <exact text of the MIDDLE admissible line ((N+1)//2, 1-indexed WITHIN the range), verbatim>\n   LLAST: <exact text of the LAST admissible line, verbatim — POSITIONAL, including if it is BLANK>\n   QUOTE: <one load-bearing sentence, exact, from INSIDE the admissible range, that your output above actually depends on>\n   ```\n   L1/LMID/LLAST are each a single PHYSICAL line. For a multi-span range, the admitted set is the spans concatenated in list order.\n\n'
S4='## 4 · Orchestrator-verified results (rulings whose lines also hold another location\'s items, so they are not on your list)\nEach line below was read by the orchestrator at the source line given; **no admitted line** (that physical line also carries another\nlocation\'s items); verified by the orchestrator with `sed -n \'<N>p\' DEVELOPER_RULINGS_LOG.md`. Stated without quotation marks.\n1. `DR-85` item 4, at this city: founder shares were moved at Ariun Nuur, two random shares of 3.5 to 5 points taken off Japan to Mongolia\n   and Künnarantaiga, the higher to Mongolia; the city\'s draw was redone with a new seed so that Mongolia\'s share is higher. The spec\'s\n   per-nation table holds the final figures. (source: DEVELOPER_RULINGS_LOG.md:1527 and :1528)\n2. `DR-93`: Kazakhstan entered the national pool and is placed at 1.4538% in 13 cities by the time-zone rule, Ariun Nuur among them; it is\n   not a founder here. (source: DEVELOPER_RULINGS_LOG.md:1602)\n3. `DR-100` items 1–3, every city: Hawaiian (later Tepenian) is the dominant language everywhere; a supporting tier is a language not\n   dominant but widely enough understood for business and social dealings; a city\'s supporting tier is every language group within 12\n   percentage points of its largest group (nations sharing a language summed). (source: DEVELOPER_RULINGS_LOG.md:1669)\n4. `DR-125` item 6, national by `DR-136` item 1: Hawaiian is a pidgin with a primary base of English and Japanese and a supporting base of\n   Korean, Tagalog, Ilocano and Mexican Spanish, later becoming Tepenian (one language over time). The "reviewer\'s reading" in the same\n   item (a national-vocabulary caution) is **not** the developer\'s and is not adopted (`DR-136` item 5). (source: DEVELOPER_RULINGS_LOG.md:1895, :1994)\n5. `DR-127` item 2: humans could continue arriving in Tepenia after the signing of the Falkland Treaty, and could not return to Upper\n   Earth if they did; the robot channel is open in law, with no later arrival recorded. (source: DEVELOPER_RULINGS_LOG.md:1913)\n6. **A city\'s own lines** — `DR-122` item 5: a single location\'s run takes a relation only from its own lines (source: :1868);\n   `DR-131` item 3, every city: its own files and entries, plus lines about shared things it is named in (its port, airport, road,\n   cluster), excluding any clause grounded only in another city\'s lines (source: :1949); `DR-136` item 2: a line in another city\'s pass\n   may only point to the shared thing\'s own record; item 3: the parent subnet is a shared thing the city is in, so lines about the subnet\n   count as the city\'s own, less any clause grounded only in another city\'s lines; the subnet\'s regional framework stays reserved and its\n   nickname a name only (source: :1994).\n7. **Origin and ancestry** — `DR-128` item 1: an origin row applies to both humans and robots, each at its own percentage (source:\n   :1922); `DR-130` item 7, every city: a row\'s own figure may be stated flatly and rows counted, and no characterization may rest on a\n   gap between two rows of one tier (source: :1940); `DR-131` item 7: drawn tiers stand as relabeled; no row at 4.5% or more is Notable\n   (source: :1949); `DR-136` item 10 (its general sentences): a robot who develops her or his culture within a predominantly X population\n   can reliably be considered X, though this does not always hold, so a row states the predominant one and is not a claim of purity\n   (source: :1994); `DR-137` item 7: a robot who develops her personality inside one ethnic or cultural group is of that group (source: :2003).\n8. **A pass captures a place at its height** — `DR-136` item 6: no earlier-state comparison runs inside a pass (source: :1994);\n   `DR-137` item 10: the pass derives the peak; where a place may have had several peaks, it writes an overall average of its height;\n   the road up and the decline are extrapolated later (source: :2003).\n9. **Government and justice wait for the DCI** — `DR-137` item 11: no pass writes a city\'s government form or its justice, law or law\n   enforcement until the DCI is fully complete; until then a pass writes only where decisions fall and who is present (source: :2003).\n10. **The day, the workforce, robots** — `DR-137` items 8, 9, 12: the ULM writes the day\'s structure (what it is organized around, what it\n    costs, when time is free, whom the rhythm fails), while what people do in it and human leisure wait for the CST; counting every robot\n    as workforce is the industry model\'s basis only; write a function, never a title such as "dispatcher" (source: :2003). Items 2–6\n    (robot senses vary and lean to place; a charging robot dreams, and flight mode saves siligel; cold can kill only by degrading the gel\n    brain or energy cell, no temperature written; siligel is mined, processed in some cities and its processing is exothermic; the\n    long-life tier needs access to someone skilled in robot upkeep) are national (source: :2003).\n11. **Evidence folders and the picks** — `DR-138` items 1–2, every city: the inspiration picks, the `Infrastructure/` files and the\n    city-relationship files may ground findings, citing file and line; the picks are a full generator (`G7`) for every city, each adjusted\n    to the place, some not applying (source: :2014).\n12. **Every city** — `DR-140` items 2–4: a city pass leaves its own faith and holidays to the stage after the cultural synthesis, and its\n    dishes, music, dress styles, games, slang and drinking scene to the CST, writing only what the place\'s conditions require and allow;\n    there is no national music form, music (robots\' included) is regional and derived after the DCI; a robot\'s charge draws on the electric\n    supply, warming a cold cell included, no rate written (source: :2034). `DR-141` item 3: independent convergence among unconnected\n    inspiration places is evidence; places sharing a root count once; connection must be checked (source: :2044).\n13. **`DR-142`, the input validation, 2026-11-18** (source: :2054): items 1–6 (general): 1,000 web calls per session, shared by every\n    agent; inside a pass the differentiation check runs substitutes 3 and 4 only, substitute 1 is deferred to after the pass and substitute\n    2 is not run; findings may rest on the Falkland Treaty draft\'s articles, each tagged `[treaty draft]` and listed; the treaty sheet\'s\n    pointer into one city\'s pass is dropped; `19`\'s national food figures are citable as national context only; each city\'s README lists\n    the Step −1 contract and the round records. Items 8–13 (Ariun Nuur): Step 2 reads `16` §23 L2349–2358 beside the first-pass row, §23\n    governing; spec L219\'s bullet is excluded whole as DLC and gameplay scoping; the Step 0 datasheet\'s L21 quotation is kept, flagged stale\n    in its source and not an input; Phase 5\'s flagged rows become the city\'s own citable lines, excluding clauses grounded only in another\n    city\'s lines; Phase 7\'s quotations kept, the headcount not citable, the 3.5× stale (≈ 3.8× live); the source-file errors were corrected\n    before the run.\n14. **Census II for this city:** none is computed. The census note at L675 records that this city is still absent from Census II\n    (its Census I figures new, Census II not yet computed). (source: Official_Population_Census.md:675; no admitted line — the note also\n    covers other locations; verified by the orchestrator at `sed -n \'675p\'`)\n'
